{
 "_comment": "Enchantments: charms worked into pieces at an enchanting table, for Enchanting. Every charm comes in tiers, each opened by an Enchanting level and each dearer than the last: 'level' is the Enchanting level to work it, 'xp' what it pays, 'inputs' what it costs, and 'value' what it adds to the piece's worth. The tiers of every charm are spread so that something new opens every level or two, and every tier opens at or below the Magic level it used to ask (a save from before starts its Enchanting at its Magic). A worn piece's charm adds 'bonus' or 'move_speed' at each tier; a weapon's does what 'effect' says, by 'amount'. 'slots' is what a worn piece's charm fits, 'takes' and 'fits' which weapons take a weapon's. The game builds an enchanted twin of every piece for every tier of every charm it takes -- copper_ring+keenness is the Copper Ring of Keenness I, copper_ring+keenness_2 its second tier, iron_bow+multishot_3 the Iron Bow of Multishot III -- so an enchanted piece is an item like any other (see ItemDatabase::LoadEnchantments). A higher tier of the charm a piece has raises it in place; another charm replaces it. Every charm has to be learned first: Mira teaches the first, the rest are scrolls, read from the pack like a brew's, and every tier the Enchanting level reaches comes with it. 'from' is what the table says about an unlearned one.",
